# A nested tool install is never committed: only the manifest and lock that
# pin it are. One stray vendor file used to be tracked here, which could not
# run anything and drifted from tools/phpstan/composer.lock on every install.
tools/phpstan/vendor/
# Playwright writes these on every run; they are generated artifacts, not source.
/test-results/
/playwright-report/
/blob-report/
/playwright/.cache/

# Package-discovery and framework caches: regenerated by composer install /
# artisan, so a committed copy only ever describes the machine that last wrote
# it, and a stale one breaks artisan and the test suite wherever vendor/ differs
# (PR #319 review, F-026).
#
# main carried a blanket `bootstrap/` rule here. It is deliberately NOT kept
# (PR #319 review, F-033): it would also cover bootstrap/app.php, which is
# source, and bootstrap/cache/.gitignore, which is tracked on purpose as the
# keeper that makes the directory survive a clean checkout — without it the
# framework cannot boot (F-030). Worse, it made `git add` on that keeper a
# silent no-op, so the F-030 fix could not be committed at all. The rule below
# is scoped to the generated files only: it matches neither bootstrap/app.php
# nor bootstrap/cache/.gitignore.
#
# main also carried a blanket `tools/phpstan/` rule, also deliberately not kept
# (PR #319 re-review F-063): it would hide tools/phpstan/composer.json and
# .lock, which are meant to be tracked (see that rule's own comment above). The
# scoped `tools/phpstan/vendor/` rule above already replaces it for the one
# thing that actually needs ignoring there.
/bootstrap/cache/*.php
/docs/Sargam-code-review-guideline/
/reviews/
